### 1.1 Histories, Fibers, and Observer-Relative Law

Let \(H : \mathcal{U}\) be the type of admissible interaction histories of a composite process and \(o : H \to O\) an observation map into the readings available to an observer. A reading \(y : O\) does not select a hidden state; it determines the homotopy fiber of compatible histories together with paths witnessing their agreement:

$$\mathsf{Fib}_o(y) := \sum_{h : H} (o(h) \equiv y), \qquad H \simeq \sum_{y : O} \mathsf{Fib}_o(y).$$

For any family \(P : H \to \mathcal{U}\), what the reading \(y\) warrants without probabilistic interpolation is the dependent product over the fiber:

$$\mathsf{Warrants}(y, P) := \prod_{(h, p) : \mathsf{Fib}_o(y)} P(h).$$

A global reversible law \(s : H \simeq H\) descends to an autonomous law on \(O\) if and only if there exists \(g : O \to O\) together with a homotopy \(\prod_{h:H} g(o(h)) \equiv o(s(h))\). When \(H = \mathsf{Bool} \times \mathsf{Bool}\) evolves reversibly under CNOT \(s(a,b) = (a \oplus b, b)\) and an observer reads \(o(a,b) = a\), no such \(g\) exists: \(s(\mathsf{true},\mathsf{false}) = (\mathsf{true},\mathsf{false})\) requires \(g(\mathsf{true}) \equiv \mathsf{true}\), whereas \(s(\mathsf{true},\mathsf{true}) = (\mathsf{false},\mathsf{true})\) requires \(g(\mathsf{true}) \equiv \mathsf{false}\). Apparent randomness and irreversibility arise not from stochastic collapse in \(H\), but from non-descent across \(o\).

